        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Behistun Inscription</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 522 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Authored by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, sometime between his coronation in the summer of 522 BCE and his death in autumn 486 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A rock relief and trilingual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> inscription in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a>, Elamite and Babylonian, about 15 metres high and 25 metres wide: 414 lines in five columns in Old Persian, 260 lines in eight columns in Elamite and 112 lines in Babylonian</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">On a limestone cliff at Mount Behistun (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bisotun">Bisotun</a>), near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kermanshah">Kermanshah</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, about 100 metres above an ancient road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>; an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> copy of the text was found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the longest known trilingual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> inscription; the key to deciphering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> cuneiform; Darius's account of his ancestry and of nineteen battles in a year; the list of twenty-three subject lands; the relief of Darius and the bound rebels; Henry Rawlinson's climb to copy it; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage listing in 2006</span></div>
        </div>
