    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a> had the text carved on a cliff above the road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>. It gives his ancestry, lists the lands he ruled and recounts nineteen battles fought in one year against rebels and impostors.</li><li>It is the longest known trilingual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> inscription, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a>, Elamite and Babylonian, and this made it important to the decipherment of cuneiform.</li><li>Forgotten for centuries and explained in fanciful ways, it was copied by travellers and scholars from 1598 onward, and Henry Rawlinson sent a full and accurate copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> text to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1847.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Darius's beard on the relief is a separate block of stone attached with iron pins and lead, and one of the nine bound figures appears to have been added after the others were finished.</p></aside>
    </section>

