    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">522 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Darius was crowned king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> in the summer, and the rebellions that the inscription describes followed the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">521 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Darius fought nineteen battles in a period of one year, ending in December, to put down the rebellions</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1598</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-sherley">Robert Sherley</a> saw the inscription on a diplomatic mission to Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and brought it to the attention of Western European scholars</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1604</span><span class="rf-text">Pietro della Valle visited the inscription and made preliminary drawings</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1764</span><span class="rf-text">Carsten Niebuhr visited the site and published a copy of the inscription in 1778</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1835</span><span class="rf-text">Henry Rawlinson began studying the inscription in earnest and later scaled the cliff to copy the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> text</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1847</span><span class="rf-text">Rawlinson sent a full and accurate copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> inscription to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2006</span><span class="rf-text">The site became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-i.jpg" alt="Darius I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rumlu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The inscription belongs to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a> (r. 522–486 BCE), who was crowned king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> in the summer of 522 BCE. It stands on Mount Behistun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kermanshah">Kermanshah</a> Province, near the city of Kermanshah in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> name of the place was Bagastana, meaning "the place of god". The cliff rises above an ancient road that connected the capitals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> and Media, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>, so that travellers on one of the main routes of the region passed beneath it.</p><p>The place had a long history before Darius. Archaeological evidence indicates that the region became a human shelter 40,000 years ago, and the Behistun complex covers 116 hectares. The relief also had models. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anubanini">Anubanini</a> rock relief at Sarpol-e Zahab, made for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lullubi">Lullubi</a> king Anubanini and dated to about 2300 BCE, lies not far away and resembles it closely in the attitude of the ruler, the trampling of an enemy and the lines of prisoners. It is speculated that the sculptors of Behistun had probably seen it, and the Lullubian reliefs are described as the model for Darius's. The inscriptional tradition of the Achaemenids, especially from Darius I, is thought to derive from the traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, Lullubi, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii.jpg" alt="Cambyses II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stela_Cambyses_Apis_closeup.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jona Lendering, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The inscription opens with a brief autobiography of Darius, in which he names his father Hystaspes and traces his descent through Arsames, Ariaramnes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teispes">Teispes</a> to Achaemenes. He states that this is why the family are called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a>, that eight of his dynasty were kings before him and that he is the ninth, and that by the grace of Ahuramazda he is king. A later passage lists the countries subject to him, twenty-three lands in all, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, Media, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, Sogdia, Gandhara and Scythia.</p><p>The longest part is an account of the events that followed the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a>. Darius states that he fought nineteen battles in one year, ending in December 521 BCE, to put down rebellions across the empire. In his account the revolts were organised by several impostors and their co-conspirators in various cities, each of whom falsely proclaimed himself king. Darius presents himself as victorious in every battle and credits his success to the grace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a>.</p><p>The text is illustrated by a life-sized bas-relief of Darius holding a bow as a sign of kingship, with his left foot on the chest of a figure lying on its back, reputed to be the pretender <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Gaumata</a>. Two servants attend him on the left. On the right stand nine one-metre figures with hands tied and ropes round their necks, representing conquered peoples, and a Faravahar floats above, giving its blessing to the king.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-ii.jpg" alt="Darius II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-ii">Darius II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_II_(reduced_shadow).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diego Delso, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Darius authored the inscription sometime between his coronation in the summer of 522 BCE and his death in the autumn of 486 BCE. It is written in three languages, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a>, Elamite and Babylonian, a variety of Akkadian. The Old Persian text has 414 lines in five columns, the Elamite text 260 lines in eight columns and the Babylonian text 112 lines. The whole composition, text and relief, measures about 15 metres in height and 25 metres in width, and it was cut into a limestone cliff about 100 metres above the road.</p><p>The relief shows signs of having been altered during the work. One of the nine bound figures appears to have been added after the others were completed, and so was Darius's beard, which is a separate block of stone attached with iron pins and lead. The inscription depicts no battle directly and describes the campaigns with a formulaic brevity. As in earlier inscriptions, it stresses the connection between victory and divine approval, and its selective geographical patterns, dates and statistics serve to enhance its credibility. A copy of the text in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, written during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-ii">Darius II</a>, was found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bisotun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bisotun.jpg" alt="Bisotun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bisotun">Bisotun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bistoon_Karvansary._plane_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Peymanpakzad, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid dynasty</a> and its successors, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> fell out of use, the nature of the inscription was forgotten and fanciful explanations became the norm. The name Behistun comes from Ancient Greek and Arabic sources, in particular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> and Ya'qubi, and was transliterated into English in the 19th century by Henry Rawlinson. The modern Persian name is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bisotun">Bisotun</a>.</p><p>In 1598 the Englishman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-sherley">Robert Sherley</a> saw the monument during a diplomatic mission to Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> on behalf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and brought it to the attention of Western European scholars. His party wrongly concluded that it was Christian in origin. The French general Gardanne thought it showed Christ and his twelve apostles, and Sir Robert Ker Porter thought it represented the Lost Tribes of Israel and Shalmaneser of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. In 1604 the Italian explorer Pietro della Valle visited and made preliminary drawings.</p><p>Scholarly study followed. Carsten Niebuhr, a German surveyor travelling for Frederick V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, visited around 1764 and published a copy in 1778. Georg Friedrich Grotefend and others used his transcriptions in trying to decipher Old Persian cuneiform, and by 1802 Grotefend had deciphered ten of its 37 symbols, after realising that Old Persian, unlike the Semitic cuneiform scripts, is alphabetic and separates its words with a slanted vertical sign.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The inscription has survived on its cliff, but its condition has changed. Rainwater has dissolved some areas of the limestone in which the text was cut, while leaving new deposits of limestone over other areas and covering the text. In the 20th century the monument suffered some damage from Allied soldiers using it for target practice in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-soviet-invasion-of-iran">Anglo-Soviet invasion of Iran</a>. In 1938 the Nazi German think tank Ahnenerbe took an interest in the inscription, but its research plans were cancelled because of the outbreak of the war.</p><p>Rawlinson began studying it in earnest in 1835 and, with the help of a local boy, scaled the cliff despite its relative inaccessibility to copy the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> text. The Elamite lay across a chasm and the Babylonian four metres above, both beyond easy reach, and he left them for later. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> expedition in 1904, led by Leonard William King and Reginald Campbell Thompson, and a University of Michigan expedition in 1948 under George G. Cameron obtained photographs, casts and more accurate transcriptions, including passages that Rawlinson had not copied.</p><p>In 1999 Iranian archaeologists began to document and assess the damage of the 20th century, using photogrammetry with two cameras to make three-dimensional images. Conservation work followed, and the site became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2006. In 2012 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bisotun">Bisotun</a> Cultural Heritage Center organised an international effort to re-examine the inscription.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The inscription became important to the decipherment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> because it is the longest known trilingual cuneiform text. The same account stands in three languages, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> version, which Rawlinson copied and sent in full to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1847, gave scholars a text they could work from. Because the town's name was anglicised as Behistun at the time Rawlinson began his study in 1835, the monument became known as the Behistun Inscription.</p><p>Its text is also a source for the history of the early reign of Darius. It records the ancestry he claimed, the lands he ruled and his own version of the rebellions after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a>. Scholars have continued to study it, with new readings and editions published in recent years of its Elamite version and of one of its paragraphs, and with one hypothesis that treats it as an imperial calendar.</p><p>The Behistun complex holds eighteen other historical monuments registered in the Iranian national list of historical sites. Paul J. Since its listing as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2006 the inscription has remained a subject of conservation and research.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Behistun Inscription is a trilingual rock inscription and relief that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a> had cut into a cliff in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, recounting his ancestry and his nineteen battles against rebels in one year. It was copied by European travellers and scholars from 1598 and was important to the decipherment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>.</em></p>

